WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
Track and Field 2, forget the gold and go for the endorsement bucks.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:27.660
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.660
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.340
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.080
And what is the back of that box?

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:34.100
So thank you.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:41.600
Thank you. Because that's literally my first talking point is like, I genuinely have no idea what the back of the box is trying to tell us.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.480
Because I guess I'm interested in that game, right?

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:53.040
A game where you play as an athlete whose main objective is just to make money at any cost and skill is just secondary to how big of a contract they can land.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:54.840
Yeah, but it's not this game.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.020
That's definitely not this game.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.820
There's no, there's no management.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.920
You represent your country in what we know as the Olympics.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:09.580
And, and you just, you just either win gold medals or you don't, you don't gain money for winning those gold medals.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:24.960
I guess they're just, uh, they're, they're saying you should imagine all of the, uh, prospective opportunities for advertising that you would get, um, had you got, gotten these gold medals.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.400
I feel like they're talking to the wrong market though.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.500
I feel like the, you know, the, the kids that are likely buying this game are, don't care too much about endorsement deals.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.940
I don't know. I mean, I'm sure every, I wanted to be a Nike boy.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:41.400
I wanted my own pair of air, air Sean's.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:43.720
Maybe it's marketed to you.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.500
Maybe they should imply that though.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:57.680
Like, uh, you know, like at the end of the Olympic mode, there should be a thing where it's like, uh, you just signed a major contract with insert made up, you know, or just Konami in general.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.180
Like, you know, they're the ones that made the game.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.500
Like now you signed a lucrative deal with Konami because you're so good at sports.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:09.520
Like I would be appreciative, uh, or understanding a little more of the back of the box, but I am genuinely confused.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.160
However, that's not what we're going to focus on.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.900
We're going to move on into track and field.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:32.400
You know, this is track and field to TF2, not to be confused with team fortress two, but track and field was an NES game that we played kind of like maybe I want to say almost a hundred episodes ago now, uh, maybe even more.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.100
And that was, you know, kind of the same thing as what track and field two is.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.080
Track and field two, of course, you know, bigger, better, better graphics, uh, more, more modes to play and everything like that.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.100
But really, really though, what is new?

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:51.660
What makes track and field two, uh, what's the call to attention for you guys?

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.180
Why all the new games, Mike?

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.080
Such as Sean?

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:02.480
Well, you got fencing, um, which I don't think we've seen before.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:10.080
Never. You've got archery, which I think we might have seen, but maybe, maybe not.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.220
You got hang gliding like that's, that's crazy.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:19.660
Uh, and then you've got some other stuff that we've kind of seen in one form or another.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.820
Uh, okay, but forgive me, is hang gliding an Olympic event?

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:32.800
Um, I, well, they say in the manual that it's an exhibition, uh, which means that it's not really graded or, or, or a competition.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.820
It's just like, Hey, look, this guy can hang glide.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:37.180
Isn't that interesting?

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.240
Um, and then you do that.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.060
I guess that's fine.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:46.240
You know, track and field two is because track and field was a game too, that never really presented itself around the Olympics.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:55.160
So I guess the Olympics conceit here is why I'm questioning things like hang gliding, uh, or even arm wrestling in the versus mode.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.100
There's an arm wrestling, uh, mode.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.180
Uh, you know, sure.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:00.520
Like these things could be Olympic sports one day for all we know.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:08.940
So maybe Konami is just thinking ahead of the time, but Joe, is there anything else that makes this like more enticing than just like track and field?

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:09.880
But with more mini games?

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:24.380
I mean, I think that it seems like the intent was to make it a little more robust, at least as far as the controls and, and, um, you know, the, the graphics and just the overall presentation.

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:30.340
Uh, and I think it's like some extent it, I want to say it succeeded.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:33.420
I'll admit that I did not go back and play the original track and field.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:51.020
So there's some, some, not all, but some of these, uh, events surprised me with like being, uh, like being more, I don't know, more involved and more, um, I don't know, doable, I guess.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.320
Some of them. Then, then I remember other games like this.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:59.100
I can't remember if I'm specifically thinking of track and field, but other games like this that we've had with like several sporting events.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:15.380
Like world games and, and yeah, the original track and field, like the one thing that I associate with these is that they're all some variation on hit a button really fast to, uh, accumulate like, uh, speed or stamina.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:34.520
Um, and that sort of like, is the, the main conceit of the game where as that still exists in track and field too, but like some of them like canoeing, like there's actual like navigation and, um, it's not just a button masher in that sense.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:42.140
And, uh, there's a little bit more to these games than in previous sporting competition NES games.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:49.420
Yeah. Well, one thing I remember from the original track and field is that was, that was a game that felt very polished to me.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:51.700
If, if not just, you know, maybe too basic.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:56.820
Um, and again, that has to go to, you know, the, the controls being limited at the time.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:04.660
I do think the solve here in track and field too, uh, you know, to, to make the button inputs a little more like involved.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:07.880
You know, I think I look at like even triple jump, right in triple jump.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:15.660
That could just be a thing where you just like tap the A button really fast and then press up on the D pad to jump and then jump again and then jump again.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:27.440
But they managed to make it like, you know, a little more, um, there's a lot, there's a lot more that you're required to do to land these things without fouling or without breaking your momentum.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:31.840
And I think like these kinds of games need something like that, right?

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.300
They need to be a little more involved because there's only two buttons on this controller.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:45.680
So you got to thread the needle between being like too easy and it doesn't even matter to like go back and replay it or, you know, being too hard.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:51.780
And then it's like figure skating, uh, on winter games where it's like, I don't even know if I'm actually doing anything.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:54.360
Like no matter what I do, she falls on the ice.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:07:05.400
You know, it was like this game manages to make each one of these mini games, something that is like a completely different challenge from the ones before it, even the way they space them out in the Olympic mode.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:12.360
Right. You're not, you're not just like doing all the running events at once and then all of the shooting events at once.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:24.480
Like they, they do space it out nicely and it makes for, um, you know, it makes it like, okay to do poorly in like a particular event and then make up for it in the next one.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:28.100
And there's something exciting about that where, you know, you're not out of it.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:37.020
If you, if you don't do something a hundred percent, you can, you can come back like, oh, I, I messed up my triple jump, but now I'm going into freestyle swimming and I feel better about that.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:40.600
You know, I'm not saying that that's true, but that could happen in this kind of game.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:59.940
And so I think the, the constant requiredness of just, you know, your, your ability to react to the new games as you're learning them, but then even going back to them, it's like you have to reset your brain each time and try these new games that it doesn't feel so much like a bunch of separate mini games, but more like one giant Olympic contest.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:09.960
Yeah. And there's also the, like the element of, you know, trying to translate, trying to translate something that in real life I imagine is very difficult.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:17.840
Like the triple jump in the, in the Olympics would, you know, be very tough to do that to a, to a control pad with six buttons.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:20.820
Like it's almost inherently going to be easy on the NES.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:38.500
So like you need to add in those little, like those little like complicated, uh, like rules to it that, that you were talking about a minute ago to kind of make it feel, feel more challenging and, and give you like a reason to, to have to practice to improve.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.960
And I feel like most of the games are like that.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:54.420
Like none of them are just like, like, I don't think any of them are super, super counterintuitive to a point where it's a negative, but none of them are like so intuitive that you would think like the way you would think of other NES games.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:09:00.380
Like you get into the canoe and it's not like, okay, A is to go forward and you press left and you turn left and you press right and you turn right.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:04.460
There's like more to it than that because actual canoeing is not that easy.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:08.180
You know what I mean? So it gives you a reason to like have to practice each event.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:14.020
I think that the one thing that, that makes it a little different is that if you're in the Olympics, you're not doing every single one of these events.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:21.720
So, so if you want to do well at this game, you need to practice, you know, what, like 16 events and get good at them, which probably would take some time.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:32.160
But, uh, but yeah, you really, you really do need to practice because like you, you, you mentioned Mike that like none of them are too easy, but, but also none of them are too hard.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:38.140
I'd say that while the, the concepts you're, you're trying to grasp in any of these aren't all that hard.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:50.340
Some of them are incredibly difficult just from like that button mashing that I mentioned earlier, like the, the, the speed at which you have to button mash it in some of these modes is kind of ridiculous.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:10:06.980
And, uh, you will like sort of, if you're playing the Olympic mode or the career mode where it goes, it goes through them in order and you, and you don't do well enough in like the triple jump, like you have to, you have to start over like from the beginning.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:13.820
Um, so it's not really that, um, uh, I wouldn't say it's, it's very forgiving in that sense.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:18.640
Well, that being said, it does have like a practice mode where you're basically playing the same thing.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.100
Uh, it's not, it's just not like a sudden death situation.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:36.660
Um, and you should probably use the practice mode quite a lot if you're trying to be good at this game, but, uh, a lot of it requires some obscene finger speed that might break some controllers, I think.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:40.700
Or might have in, uh, when, when this game was popular.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:45.740
Yeah. I wonder about that power meter, uh, that's built into this game.

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:55.060
I, I've never really met anything like it, uh, since, you know, even in Mario party games, there's nothing where like you tap the A button as fast as I think.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:56.620
I'm thinking I'm a pretty fast tapper.

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:59.540
I never saw this thing go to a full bar.

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:02.360
And I'm wondering like, is that by design?

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:06.480
Is that supposed to be that like you, you can't max out this power bar?

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.960
Did you guys have any luck at maxing out that power bar?

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:17.840
And did you find that maybe a little effective that it had this like threshold that like, even though you're going so fast, you still see that you're not using all out power.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.220
Was that an effective, um, measurement for you as you're playing the games?

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:28.520
Well, uh, yeah, I will say that I, I was, I would be impressed with myself if I got it even close to like three quarters of the way to the end.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:33.500
Like it, I'm not even sure I ever got it to three quarters, but like definitely never got past that.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:39.560
And definitely never, well, I should say in most of the events, I think there was one or two events where I was able to fill up that bar.

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:46.220
I think, um, I think, uh, the hammer throw, which you're not just rapidly pressing one button.

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:48.820
You're pressing like four buttons in consecutive order.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:52.860
That one I got closer, but for the most part, it was like really, really difficult.

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:56.800
Um, and I, it does make you feel like you can always go faster.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:12:05.180
So, I mean, I, I wouldn't be surprised if it was by design that it's like, maybe, maybe not even that it's like coded in there to be like impossible for it to get to the end.

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:11.600
But maybe it's like actually just physically impossible to hit the button enough times a second to get it to go to the end.

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:15.540
Yeah, I think you definitely need a turbo to, to do that consistently.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:30.160
Um, and, uh, what, one thing that I'll say right off the bat is that the, the power meter or like just the, um, how fast you, cause it isn't the same sort of stamina thing.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:47.980
Um, as it is in some other modes, but, um, the, the freestyle swimming, I, when I was playing the sort of career mode, like that's where I had to tap out and move over to practice because I could not for the life of me, uh, qualify for that mode.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:54.260
Uh, because of just like the intense amount of button mashing you have to do.

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:56.880
Uh, it's, it was, it was insane.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:58.800
Yeah. You're not alone there.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:00.880
I couldn't even come close in the freestyle swimming.

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:08.160
I mean, the guy would laugh me every time that the opponent, I did get to a point where he would like, I was competing with the opponent.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:15.940
I won. I, I, I beat him twice and it was still, it was still slower than the qualification.

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:18.100
It was, I don't know what it wanted me to do.

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:19.560
Beat him by a, by a lap.

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:24.600
There's something I'm sure, I'm sure you've done this too.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.120
If you beat the computer though.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:35.700
Um, when you enter the pool from the, uh, you know, from the gun going off, you can choose which kind of, um, swim style you want to do.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:42.340
And I believe you have a choice between the, um, freestyle and the, is it butterfly?

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:43.940
Is that what it is? Yeah, there's butterfly.

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:50.140
And then I don't know if it's freestyle, but it's a, it's the more standard, like what you think of when you think of swimming.

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:51.580
Um, right.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.660
Okay. Yeah. Um, and yeah.

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:54.780
I don't know what that's called to be honest.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:14:01.360
I forgot it's in the manual, but yeah, the one is faster, but it depletes your oxygen faster as well.

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.160
So you have to have more careful timing with that.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:06.900
And it didn't seem to matter like which one I use.

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:12.620
Like if you, cause if you use the butterfly, you're really playing with fire because you can just run out of breath.

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:15.400
And if you run out of breath once you've lost.

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:20.820
So, uh, there was, it's a very tough unforgiving mode.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:30.720
Right. Which is weird to go up against, uh, you know, something like that where you have before that you had fencing and triple jump that I'm not saying they were easy.

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:39.880
You know, like fencing is something that theoretically, like if you're not, if you're just pushing buttons and spamming attacks, like I'm, I'm certain that the computer could take you down.

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:41.340
You'd have to think about it a little bit.

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:50.960
And with the triple jump, it's not easy to, uh, you know, jump super far, but the qualifiers aren't like so ridiculous that you don't make it through.

00:14:51.480 --> 00:15:06.320
Yes. So, so it is weird to have that, um, with the swimming requirement, but I think for the most part, you know, even if you just, even if you only played through each event, like just, you know, thinking about today's and like how much time we have and stuff like that.

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:10.120
If you only played these in practice mode and just selected a few of them, right.

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:13.240
The ones that you like, or the ones that we feel respond.

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:20.260
Well, I, I don't know if like all 16 of these have to be perfect for me to consider this like a good game.

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:28.800
Well, well, here's, here's my question.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:35.080
How many of these did you find yourself having a great time with or a good time?

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.580
Like how many of these did you have like genuine fun with?

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:39.120
I, I'm a little biased here.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:40.680
You know, I did like track and field.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:48.140
So, uh, you know, I, I, I enjoy these, these little tests of strength where, uh, you have to master the inputs a little bit.

00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:55.380
I would say that outside of probably the freestyle swimming, high dive, maybe archery.

00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:58.900
I think the other ones, I, I think I enjoyed them for the most part.

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:02.400
Really? Archery is actually one of the ones that I, that I liked quite a bit.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:05.260
Yeah. That's actually one of the ones that I would say I had a lot of fun with.

00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:11.260
I think I just had problems with the, the power meter and rapidly tapping a and releasing.

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:14.280
Um, I think I just had problem with that for me.

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:18.720
Like it was a little more involved than the way that it works for say triple jump, but.

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:23.220
Were you aiming up when, when the, when the target got further away?

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:24.720
When it was getting further away.

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:26.260
Yes. No. Yeah. When it was, yeah.

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:27.380
When it was further away. Absolutely.

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:29.780
It's just also, I couldn't quite tell.

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:36.780
Sometimes my arrow looked like it was at least on the, you know, like it was parallel with

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:42.000
the final, you know, where, with the board, but it just said miss, you know, like it didn't,

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:43.680
it didn't connect for whatever reason.

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:47.260
And I didn't think that like I could be too far to the left or right.

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:52.600
I was wondering if anybody else experienced that, uh, not that it went over or didn't connect

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:57.320
with it. It looked like it almost went through it and it just said, maybe that is a missing on the

00:16:57.320 --> 00:16:58.520
left or right sort of thing.

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:08.420
Yeah. Uh, to, to anyone who has not played this game, archery, uh, is basically just like lining

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:10.100
up an artillery shot.

00:17:10.580 --> 00:17:13.900
Um, and that's, that's pretty much it.

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:17.380
Uh, that's, that's how I would describe it in terms of how it controls.

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:23.800
Yeah. But then you, you rapidly have to press a, in order to pull the bow back or whatever.

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:27.040
Yeah. And, uh, and like that gives you your power.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:30.260
So it does that same thing where it starts to max out after a while when you can't pull

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:35.220
any further. And I think it actually does like a, a, a good balance of like how difficult it would

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:39.560
be to, cause like, you know, I did okay until I was like the, what is it?

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:41.460
90 meters was the last one.

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:43.580
And that was the one where it was like, okay, now it's really challenging.

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:45.160
Like 70 was giving me some trouble.

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:46.440
90 was really challenging.

00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:51.080
And that was the last one. So I felt like it was like a pretty good, um, like scale of difficulty.

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:56.860
Yeah. So which other events would you call out Joe that you didn't like?

00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:02.360
I mean, look, I don't want to go as far to say I didn't like all of these other ones.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:04.960
I mean, some of them I enjoyed while I was doing, but I'm not like thinking like, wow,

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:06.700
that was fun. I would like to do it again.

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:11.520
You know, it was like, oh, it was cool to kind of learn it to Matt, to like master it

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:13.400
or not, and then move on.

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:17.160
But like, uh, like, all right, Taekwondo.

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:19.120
Taekwondo is jank.

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:23.160
It was cool for a second until I realized I could just kick the hell out of the opponent

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:24.580
just over and over again.

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:26.620
Just no, with no consequences at all.

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:32.020
Like if you just hit the kick button, the straight middle kick over and over again, you win.

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:34.800
So that, that was kind of broken for me.

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:36.920
I was getting some random trips when I did that.

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:39.340
I don't know. Yeah.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:42.800
I think like once in a while he would hit me from doing that, but for the most, like,

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:48.700
but like for every, for every five times I would beat him, he would knock me down.

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:50.140
So like I would win regardless.

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:57.360
Um. I wasn't sure like what qualifies enough of like a depletion for them to, for them or you

00:18:57.360 --> 00:18:58.380
to be knocked down.

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:02.340
I felt like I was doing a pretty good job and then all of a sudden they would connect

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:04.840
like one hit and I'd be like down.

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:09.240
And I don't think you, you have any control over like getting back up.

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:12.000
Um, I tried rapidly pressing buttons and stuff like that.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:18.980
I'm not entirely sure. It's one of the, it's one of the mini games that seems to have like the least amount of,

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:20.480
uh, feedback.

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:27.540
Like everything else, not everything else, but, uh, most of these games are so systematized

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:30.800
that you, you, you have like perfect information.

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:34.680
You know what you're doing, you know what you're doing wrong most of the time.

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:42.040
But, uh, and even in the, the other, uh, the competitive game where you're actually playing

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:49.140
against an artificial intelligence, um, fencing, like it, it's pretty clear when you've been

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:55.820
struck. Uh, whereas with Taekwondo, uh, it's not even just that you've been struck.

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:59.940
It's also that you've been struck hard enough to fall.

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:07.500
And then it also doesn't really give you any indication of like what, um, what's keeping

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:09.180
you from getting back up.

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:14.640
Uh, so it's just a very, it's, it's a much more foggy game mode than the rest of them.

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:18.740
Um, yeah, it feels like they probably should have put like all of their effort into developing

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:23.040
fencing and then not worry about making another game that's kind of too similar because it

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:26.160
feels like they tried to make it different enough and make different, you know, make

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:30.360
different like rules to it and it, and it just didn't end up working.

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:35.740
But I'll say real quick that I think that, um, the triple jump, freestyle swimming, high

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:44.500
diving, Taekwondo, uh, the, maybe the pole vault, uh, all were not particularly fun for me.

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:49.160
Like, like, like a lot of those would be like, oh, it was fun to, for me to like, oh, I figured

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:52.420
it out. I got the timing down and I'm like, well, do I really, now I got the timing down.

00:20:52.540 --> 00:20:55.280
Like, I don't know, is this fun?

00:20:55.460 --> 00:20:58.660
And for me, those were not, they were either frustrating or boring.

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:04.200
The other ones were a mix of passable and some that I actually really liked.

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:07.060
The pole vault was close for me, Joe.

00:21:07.340 --> 00:21:09.020
Uh, I, I hated that at first.

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:13.880
Um, it just takes some time to figure out getting that pole in the exact spot.

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:18.720
You know, I, I wish they would have opened up the window on when you can first stick the

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:24.300
pole up because I feel like the rest of that, like once you're up there and like how much

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:29.600
you bend it and how much you choose to go over it and when you choose to, to release, like

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:30.620
that's all cool.

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:34.140
And actually pretty authentic to like the things that matter in pole vaulting.

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:35.600
So I was on board with it.

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:38.520
I wish they would have opened up that window a little bit, but once I nailed that timing

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:42.680
on when to, to put down the pole, I felt pretty good about that particular mode.

00:21:42.820 --> 00:21:44.600
I felt the same way with the pole vaulting.

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:46.500
Once I figured it out, I was like, oh, okay.

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:47.860
It was satisfying for me to figure it out.

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:49.000
Now I'm like, now this makes sense.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:52.200
But like, I just didn't find it like repeatable fun to me.

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:55.780
Even once I had figured it out, just like the button mashing.

00:21:55.980 --> 00:22:02.120
I do wish that, that pretty much every game in this, in this didn't have to involve button

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:05.380
mashing. Like it's a little, a little much, like I get it.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:06.420
You need to, you need that skill.

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:10.520
But like that button mashing, like honestly kind of, kind of wears me down after a while.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:11.960
I'm like, I'm sick of button mashing.

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:16.760
Yeah, I think like in terms of the button mashing, if they just change it to like, I don't know,

00:22:17.140 --> 00:22:23.600
like a rhythm kind of thing, like you do had to, you had to still have some precision and

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:29.940
still have some speed in how you were like repeatedly, repeatedly hitting a button, but

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:35.540
maybe you have to hit it in the, like the right rhythm so that like each time you hit that

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:41.560
consecutively, you get a bit more power so that it's not just brute strength, uh, or who

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:45.240
has a turbo button that can actually do these things.

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:49.520
And it would, I think it would be more interesting if it was like that instead of just going,

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:51.940
but I don't know. Right.

00:22:51.940 --> 00:22:57.040
I feel like a lot of these button mashing games probably benefit more from, um, you know,

00:22:57.480 --> 00:22:58.740
the, uh, what's that called?

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:02.360
The, the NES controller that looks like an arcade controller.

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:04.640
Um, the advantage, the NES advantage.

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:09.400
Like if you had that, I feel like almost all of these modes, even think about fencing or

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:11.940
Taekwondo, like I feel like they would benefit from it too.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:15.800
There's something about, well, at least what I do with a controller when I'm doing these

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:20.920
button mashing games about laying it down, you know, and, and having more control over

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:25.180
mashing the buttons than just using your particular thumb, like being able to strike with your index

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:26.400
just feels good.

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:28.440
But then the D-pad doesn't feel as good.

00:23:28.580 --> 00:23:33.300
So that's why the advantage probably would have came into, uh, a little more use there because

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:38.900
then you have more control also over, uh, the, the D-pad inputs, which for something

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:40.940
like hammer throw would have went a long way, right?

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:47.380
I mean, spinning around in a circle on the D-pad to, to get your character winding up and,

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:51.680
and being prepared to release the hammer would have been great with an analog stick.

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:56.720
Yeah. I actually switched to a PlayStation controller and use the analog stick and then it was much,

00:23:56.860 --> 00:23:57.760
much, much easier.

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:02.460
I had to put all the, uh, inputs for the D-pad onto the analog stick.

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:05.180
You were just emulating an NES advantage.

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:06.700
That's all. Yeah, exactly.

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:14.500
All right, but let's talk for a second.

00:24:14.600 --> 00:24:17.440
Let's get away from the breaking down the individual games here.

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:25.500
I feel like this is presentationally and, and specifically graphically one of the best looking

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:27.160
NES games we've ever seen.

00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:31.920
Yeah, I'd say I really liked the look of some of these games.

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:33.940
I don't know about like best ever.

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:43.340
Um, maybe, maybe in terms of like, uh, the detail of, uh, the characters in it, then, then

00:24:43.340 --> 00:24:44.300
yeah, you're right.

00:24:44.460 --> 00:24:46.120
These are pretty well done.

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:53.680
Um, but, uh, yeah, especially the, uh, the, this, this rubber, not rubber.

00:24:54.700 --> 00:24:56.220
What's the thing that you're shooting?

00:24:56.440 --> 00:25:02.440
The clay pigeons, uh, that, that game or that mini game, like was probably the best looking,

00:25:02.680 --> 00:25:07.720
uh, it had like some parallax action going when you're moving left and right.

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:14.600
Uh, there, there was a sense of depth with, um, even your reticle as it was moving around.

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:16.040
I really liked the look of that one.

00:25:16.040 --> 00:25:17.540
So yeah. And that was a fun one too.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:21.780
That was that, you know, anything that, anything that makes me think of duck hunt is always

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:29.920
fun. And one where using the D pad to, to be your, you know, like your zapper in that particular,

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:32.020
it wasn't weird. Right.

00:25:32.020 --> 00:25:33.900
And I think that was in the original track and field.

00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:36.280
Wasn't it something similar that we didn't hate also?

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:37.880
I believe so.

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:44.500
So tried and true. Uh, you know, what's funny though, is there is another, um, gun game in this.

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:50.200
I think it's only, uh, it's only a bonus event, but, uh, that one lets you use the controller

00:25:50.200 --> 00:25:54.400
just like how you can use it for the clay pigeon shooting, but it also lets you use the NES

00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:56.260
zapper if you have one plugged in.

00:25:56.440 --> 00:25:59.060
And I wonder why, and maybe, maybe it does.

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:01.460
I just didn't check it out because the manual didn't mention it.

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:06.300
I wonder why they didn't also make it possible for clay pigeon shooting to also have the,

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:08.100
uh, the zapper be compatible.

00:26:08.660 --> 00:26:12.340
Huh? Yeah, that would have been, that would have been a nice, a nice touch.

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:16.520
Uh, and I will also say about the, uh, the gun firing little bonus game.

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:20.860
I'm pretty sure this is not an Olympic sport in which you're just like on the streets of

00:26:20.860 --> 00:26:25.200
New York and a bunch of like shady looking criminal people come out with guns and you

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:26.160
actually shoot them.

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:28.880
There's like an open trunk of a car in the background.

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:33.640
Yeah. I'm not sure what they were thinking with some of these, uh, exhibitions and bonus modes,

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:35.820
but you know what? They're fun and they're harmless, right?

00:26:36.020 --> 00:26:39.560
Uh, at the end of the day, it's Konami just, just tapping into their silliness a little bit.

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:44.040
Going back to presentation for a moment, you know, like, you know, it's great that we

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:46.360
highlighted some of the, you know, the graphical prowess of it.

00:26:46.360 --> 00:26:53.580
And it is weird that, you know, certain mini games have like maybe more detail or, um, look

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:57.140
better than other ones, but humans do look really good in this one.

00:26:57.240 --> 00:26:58.060
And it is funny.

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:02.920
I know you guys said you didn't go back to track and field, but if you do go back and

00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:08.220
just even put like two screenshots side by side, the, you would think they were on different

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:09.540
consoles. Yeah.

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:11.240
I'm looking at it right now.

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:15.080
Yeah. Just so rushed, I guess, to, to get that onto the NES.

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:17.940
And now they had time with track and field too.

00:27:17.940 --> 00:27:24.600
It's, it really is, it really does make the NES seem more capable in terms of what it can

00:27:24.600 --> 00:27:30.500
do, uh, against, I guess, like, you know, the, the Sega Genesis is now, um, coming out

00:27:30.500 --> 00:27:31.520
in late 1989.

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:39.000
So maybe, maybe like now graphics are on people's minds because Sega is advertising 16 bit, but

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:45.280
even just, even just like the idea of like your plane pulling up and every, and like the,

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:51.500
the details of that and the, the jumbotron with the, um, opening ceremony and you see

00:27:51.500 --> 00:27:56.740
the faces as they're like on the parade, the, the typewriter that types up your results.

00:27:56.900 --> 00:28:01.540
I mean, I know that like, it's just text for the most part, but that typewriter is beautiful.

00:28:01.540 --> 00:28:04.360
I'd play a typewriter game on the NES if it looked like that.

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:05.520
You know, I take it back.

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:09.940
I, I kind of like doubted you when you were saying that this is one of the better looking

00:28:09.940 --> 00:28:11.280
games we played. You're right.

00:28:11.280 --> 00:28:18.260
Uh, but looking at like, even on the jumbotron and you get, um, each of your, I don't know

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:24.200
if it's like unique to, I know that you're playing as a different person in each game.

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:29.940
Uh, but is it the, is it a different portrait also depending on like your nationality?

00:28:30.060 --> 00:28:36.520
Like it, it, that all of those different portraits are really cool looking to with like the, um,

00:28:36.520 --> 00:28:39.780
um, limited color space that they have.

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:42.540
Um, they're very dramatic looking.

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:48.580
Yes. And it highlights like the name of your, you know, like the United States fencer.

00:28:48.940 --> 00:28:53.020
Yeah. Like each, each one has like its own individual identity too, which like, even though it doesn't

00:28:53.020 --> 00:28:59.680
mean much, right. I don't think the country you choose, uh, performs better at events that maybe they historically

00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:01.180
performed, uh, well at.

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:04.180
That would have been interesting to add like another layer of depth.

00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:08.620
I don't know if we needed that given how many, how much of the game relies on like how good

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:09.940
you are at putting in inputs.

00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:14.040
I don't think we needed any like additional skill barriers depending on which country you

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:19.100
pick to be added, but you bring up a good point, Sean, of like, there is some form of

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:22.320
identity, even if it is just being able to choose a country.

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:25.360
Remember, you weren't able to do that in the original track and field.

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:29.740
So having choice over, over a good amount of, of countries, but also being able to like

00:29:29.740 --> 00:29:36.200
see them in the opening ceremony and, and, um, and follow that through a court, uh, across

00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:39.360
the, um, I think it's eight days of the Olympics.

00:29:39.460 --> 00:29:44.460
I don't remember how many days it is, but, um, seeing that across, it feels a little bigger

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:45.680
of, of an adventure.

00:29:45.900 --> 00:29:51.940
Yeah. But I think, I think you didn't notice the graphics because it's not something that you

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:53.680
can really pay attention to in this game.

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:58.580
You're spending so much time putting in inputs and looking at the manual to make sure you

00:29:58.580 --> 00:30:01.000
remember how to do the mini games and then you're doing them.

00:30:01.180 --> 00:30:07.080
I don't think there's enough time to soak in say the, the, the background of the like

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:10.040
Taekwondo action and watching people in the audience, right?

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:14.320
In the audience, we're used to just seeing little heads, uh, maybe different dots.

00:30:14.540 --> 00:30:19.820
If we're lucky, sometimes this one has like, you know, uh, people of different, you know,

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:22.780
different colors and different, uh, different outfits.

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:26.880
And, uh, one guy I think is like either selling popcorn or carrying popcorn.

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:29.880
It's like, you know, like that, I like these little touches.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:31.840
Yeah. They're very nice.

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:37.800
The crowd that has like the USA signs, which like, it took me a second to realize what was

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:39.020
going on, but yeah, they're all holding it.

00:30:39.080 --> 00:30:41.080
Yeah. I thought it was sort of glitching out.

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:46.980
Me too. But it's just people holding a sign that spell out USA and good old USA purple.

00:30:54.480 --> 00:31:00.000
I know we kind of got away from, um, the, the, the negativity, uh, but I kind of want

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:01.700
to go back to the negativity again.

00:31:02.020 --> 00:31:11.940
Um, because, uh, I think that the, uh, the high dive, um, is probably one of the least

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:19.160
impressive, uh, things that you can like, nothing that you do on the high dive looks

00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:21.220
particularly graceful at all.

00:31:21.780 --> 00:31:25.200
It, it looks like you're just sort of spasming out.

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:31.300
Um, and, and it's sort of like the actual method of play isn't too far away from like

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:36.920
that wrestling game where you have to like select a move in like a turn-based mode.

00:31:36.920 --> 00:31:41.900
And it just seemed really, really, uh, janky to me.

00:31:41.900 --> 00:31:45.160
I don't know what you guys thoughts were on the high dive.

00:31:45.400 --> 00:31:46.640
Yeah. I mean, I hated that one.

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:53.180
That was, that was like the same, uh, feeling I had about it too, where it just, it's too

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:54.260
complicated. Yeah.

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:57.660
I can read the manual and it tells me what to do, but it's like a lot to remember and a

00:31:57.660 --> 00:31:58.660
lot to do all at once.

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:03.300
And like, none of it really looks cool or like the feedback is not very clear.

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:06.440
And it looks like you're always belly flopping as you go in.

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:08.400
Yes. I have no idea how to line up.

00:32:08.400 --> 00:32:09.380
I was looking for a way. Yeah.

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:12.480
Is there a way to like enter the water nicely?

00:32:12.700 --> 00:32:15.220
I wasn't sure if that was, if that was even possible.

00:32:15.460 --> 00:32:17.920
There is a way, there is a way to enter the water nicely.

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:23.580
And, and to be fair, you have to sacrifice some points by the amount of, um, the amount

00:32:23.580 --> 00:32:26.440
of moves that you do in the air in order to do that.

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:31.240
But keep in mind, the score is based primarily on those two things.

00:32:31.480 --> 00:32:34.600
One, the tricks that you do in the air and two, you're landing.

00:32:34.600 --> 00:32:41.000
So you can't like, you can't do either four tricks and try to get a perfect dive, but it

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:45.780
will affect your score if you do like four whatever tricks and then land in the water

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:49.500
with a big splash because they're going to take those points away from you anyway.

00:32:49.500 --> 00:32:54.620
So the, the best circumstance is to do like maybe, you know, two tricks, three, if you're

00:32:54.620 --> 00:32:59.900
lucky and then focus on making sure that you balance yourself out so that you're, I don't

00:32:59.900 --> 00:33:03.160
want to say you're like, you won't, you won't be a pencil per se, but your splash will

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:05.580
be noticeably smaller and you'll be rewarded for that.

00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:07.500
I got as high as like 8.8 sometimes.

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:14.060
So that is something I thought that there was only one like splash effect and it didn't

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:20.600
matter whether you completely flopped or if you got as straight as a pencil, it was always

00:33:20.600 --> 00:33:23.600
going to be this like really angular splash.

00:33:23.600 --> 00:33:27.080
But now you're telling me that you do see it differently.

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:31.900
So, cause there were like certain times where I was like not even focusing on moves.

00:33:32.060 --> 00:33:37.420
I was just trying to land correctly and it always looked like I was doing the same thing

00:33:37.420 --> 00:33:39.340
at the end and getting different scores.

